---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="ShadowADJ — about">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 leading-relaxed">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Why ShadowADJ works this way</h1>

    <p class="mt-5 text-white/70">
      ShadowADJ started from a real question: debate judges and interviewers have almost no tooling to
      help them pay attention to <em>delivery</em>. But the obvious version of that tool — one that
      listens to a live answer and outputs “this person is probably using AI” — is not something that
      can be built responsibly.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">Speech-based AI detection doesn’t work</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/70">
      Text AI-detectors already misfire often enough that major vendors have pulled or retired them,
      and they disproportionately flag non-native English speakers. Running the same idea on
      transcribed live speech is less reliable, not more. Every intuitive signal — “fewer filler
      words than usual”, “bigger vocabulary than their baseline”, “unusually well-organised” — also
      describes someone who simply prepared, rehearsed, learned formal English, or was trained in
      structured argument.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">The stakes make false positives serious</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-white/70">
      In an interview or an exam, a “78% — review” badge isn’t neutral information. It anchors the
      evaluator against the candidate before any decision is made, and the person it happens to
      usually never finds out. So ShadowADJ has no verdict output at all — not hidden, not advisory.
      The API simply cannot return one.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-xl font-semibold">What it does instead</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-white/70">
      <li>Descriptive metrics only: pace, pauses, filler rate, vocabulary variety, talk ratio, answer latency.</li>
      <li>Consent gate before any capture; the recorded person sees the same screen as everyone else.</li>
      <li>Audio held in memory and discarded at session end. No account, no server-side profile.</li>
      <li>Optional written coaching notes, addressed to the speaker, about delivery and structure.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-white/45">
      If you’re evaluating someone, ShadowADJ is an aid to your own attention — never a substitute for
      your judgement, and never evidence of misconduct.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
